From fef4d7c4c20f0bb64f96b9abf05abb75c9d5d7bb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Beany <24502088+beanyforever@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 22:18:53 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] A page read while it is saved no longer caches the copy from
 before

SiYuan's page cache holds pages as their files do, and a load that misses
it reads the page's file, then caches what it read. Nothing ordered that
store against a save of the same page: a load that read the file just
before a save wrote it could cache its copy after the save had cached its
own, leaving the cache older than the file, and the next edit, which loads
the page from the cache, saved that older copy over the file, losing the
save on disk. A page isn't cached after SiYuan starts, after a move to
another notebook drops its entries (0005), or after sync or an asset rename
rewrites its file, so a read of it while an edit to it is saved is ordinary
then. A WriteTree that finds the file already holds its bytes caches what
it read in the same way.

Both stores are now fills, as in 0004's attribute caches: the load and
WriteTree take a FillToken before their lookup and store through
FillTreeDataInBox, which, under the page cache's lock, stores only if the
page's file isn't being written and nothing wrote the page or removed it
from the cache since the token; a page too big for the cache isn't stored.
The versions are the ones 0004 keeps for block fills, per page id rather
than per notebook key, so removing a page stops a fill whatever notebook's
key it was to store under; they outlive the page's entries, and a clear
refuses every fill under way. A write's store, SetTreeDataInBox, bumps the
page's version as well as the bracket around the file write. No lock is held
across a load: a lock per page deadlocked in Go tests, against a page
rebuilding a missing parent and, beside 0004's lock, against a commit. The
page cache already keeps a generation per key against ristretto taking a
store in late, so neither store waits.

TestTreeFillRacesWrite races a LoadTree of an uncached page against a
WriteTree of it for 1,000 rounds with jitter, then makes the next edit as
SiYuan does: on v3.8.7 with 0001 to 0005 the cache was left older than the
file in 918 rounds, and the next edit lost the write from the file in each,
and with the patch in none. TestTreeFillsDuringFileWriteCacheNothing reads
the page with each fill, a LoadTree and a WriteTree of the bytes the file
holds, while a write of its file is under way, which places the read inside
the write as no race from a test can for WriteTree, whose read and store are
microseconds apart: each case fails without its own change. In the cache
package, TestTreeFillTakenBeforeWriteDoesNotStore,
TestTreeFillDuringFileWriteDoesNotStore, TestTreeFillAfterRemoveDoesNotStore
and TestTreeFillUnracedStores check FillTreeDataInBox itself: with a fill
that ignores its token, as the old store did, the first three fail in every
case, and without the check that no write is under way, the second does.

Still open: a load that fixes a damaged or older-format page's file writes
the fixed copy back unconditionally, so a save landing between its read and
that write is lost from the file. The cache keeps the save if it took the
save's store in, and then the next edit writes it back.
